# Purpose

The goal is to reduce the probability of permanent data loss as much as reasonably possible while maintaining practicality and functionality. 

# Design

I will rely on Git for version control, followed by a local backup and finally a remote backup.

For version control, I will use **Git** for data for which I want to maintain a history of changes, such as my projects, book library, and Obsidian vault. I have already been using Git for some time, and I do not see a reason to switch to another solution since Git already fulfils this role effectively.

For local backups, Git alone is not sufficient protection against system failure or other forms of data loss. I will therefore configure automatic backups of crucial data to an external drive using **Borg** and Python. This will provide a separate copy of the data that is independent of the primary filesystem.

Finally, for additional protection, I will maintain another backup at a remote location using **Proton Drive**. Since I already have a Proton subscription, Proton Drive is a natural choice for my current setup. However, once I set up my NAS as part of my networking project, I will evaluate whether hosting the remote backup on my own infrastructure is worthwhile.

I also considered using system snapshots as part of the backup strategy. However, I decided not to configure them for now because I expect to reinstall and reconfigure Arch Linux multiple times while working towards the requirements of my Linux workstation project. Once the workstation configuration becomes more stable, I intend to use **Timeshift** for system snapshots.

# Installation

## Git

Since I am using Arch Linux, I will use its `pacman` package manager to install Git:

```zsh
sudo pacman -S git
```

To verify that Git was installed correctly, I can run:

```zsh
git --version
```

If the installation was successful, this will return the installed Git version.
## Borg

The same process of installation and verification as Git:

```zsh
sudo pacman -S borg
```
## Proton-Drive-CLI

The Proton Drive CLI is not available as a package in the Arch Linux repositories, so I will follow the official documentation to install it manually.

There are two ways to do this:

### 1. Build from source

The first option is to build the CLI from source. The CLI is built using **Bun**, so I first need to install the Bun package manager and runtime:

```
sudo pacman -S bun
```

I then download the Proton Drive repository:

```
git clone https://github.com/ProtonDriveApps/sdk.git
```

I navigate to the CLI directory:

```
cd sdk/cli
```

From this directory, I can install the required dependencies and build the CLI with Bun:

```
bun install
bun run build
```

This produces a standalone executable at:

```
release/proton-drive
```

### 2. Use the provided binary

The second option is to use one of the pre-built binaries provided by Proton. For Linux x64, Proton provides a `linux/x64` build. If this build crashes at startup with an `Illegal instruction` error, Proton recommends using the `linux/x64-baseline` build instead.

After downloading the binary, I need to give it execute permission. I can do this by navigating to the directory containing the binary and running:

```zsh
chmod +x proton-drive
```

I can then move the binary to `~/.local/bin`, which allows me to execute it from any directory:

```zsh
mv proton-drive ~/.local/bin/
```

To verify that the installation was successful, I can run:

