#### First execution attempt

I initially ran the script directly:

```
~/.scripts/git_backup_script.py
```

However, it failed with errors such as:

![[Pasted image 20260924144455.png]]

The problem was not the Python code itself. The operating system was attempting to execute the file as a shell script.

Linux does not determine how to execute a script simply from its `.py` extension like in Windows. When a script is executed directly, the shebang on the first line tells the system which interpreter should be used.

I therefore added the following line to the beginning of the script:

```
#!/usr/bin/env python3
```

This tells the system to execute the file using `python3`.

After adding the shebang, I ran the script again:

![[Pasted image 20260924150006.png]]

This time it executed successfully.

